4|0@PeopleBeingJerks^1439060880#ctw5d57:BloodBlight$:Interesting read but the math looks like complete BS if you pick at it at all. They say the 85 uses 23.6kWh per 100km... But: 85kw / 270m (conservative, a 85D is rated for 295m) = 0.31kW Per Mile * 62 (about 100km) = 19.5kWh Per 100km. That is about 21% more than a conservative estimate... If you do the math for the rated millage it drops to 17.9kWh and jumps to about a 32% difference. So, if they are going to "fudge" the math on first set of numbers... Also, these comparisons never seem to take into account the energy needed to refine and transport gas. Just refining requires 4-6kWh per gallon. So, a gallon of gas is about 114k BTUs, or about 33.3kWh. So you are loosing like 20ish % just to refining. Heck, the Tesla can go 13 to 20 miles on just the energy needed to refine a gallon of gas ( 85kWh/295m*6kWh ).
3|0@PeopleBeingJerks^1439065300#ctw7l68:Perma_dude$:/u/BloodBlight pointed out a few problems with that article, but believe it or not there a couple more! * That article compares fossil fuel energy use, not carbon. * That article assumes non-baseload power, which is more polluting. But electric cars are mostly charged at night (baseload power). * That article is old, before the ["vampire drain" problem was fixed.](http://www.greencarreports.com/news/1088929_life-with-tesla-model-s-electric-draw-vampire-slain-at-last) * They fail to account for grid losses (5%) or fuel used to truck gasoline (1%). Correcting for these problems and using the [latest grid emissions data](http://www.epa.gov/cleanenergy/energy-resources/refs.html) (for 2010, the 2015 grid is cleaner) we can [calculate](http://www.wolframalpha.com/input/?i=%288.887+kg%2Fgallon+%2F+0.99+%2B+%281354.09+lb%2FMWh+%2F+0.95+*+6+kWh%2Fgallon%29%29+%2F+%28%281232.35+lb%2FMWh%29+%2F+0.95+*+%28%2885+kWh%29%2F%28270+mi%29+%2B+%281.1+kWh+year%2Fday+%2F+%2812000+mi%29%29%29%29) that the Tesla emits as much CO2 as a car getting... **63 mpg** The Honda Civic Hybrid the article used gets 45 mpg, and the US average is 25 mpg. 
5|0@PeopleBeingJerks^1439058942#ctw4dp2:BloodBlight$:That kinda depends on were you live though... Here in the NW less than 20% of our power comes from coal. On top of that, I personally (though I don't have a Tesla) generate about half of the energy I use VIA solar. Source: https://www.pacificpower.net/content/dam/pacific_power/doc/About_Us/Rates_Regulation/Washington/WA_Utility_Fuel_Mix_Report.pdf
